{"archetypes":[{"name":"cross-consumer-drift","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan updates only four files for the new ok-fallback status value but does not enumerate all consumers of REVISE_STATUS across the repo; unpatched sites could silently treat ok-fallback as a failure.","prompt_body":"The plan introduces REVISE_STATUS=ok-fallback and updates plan-review-loop.sh and plan-review.md, but does not demonstrate an exhaustive search for all sites in the repo that match on REVISE_STATUS=ok literally, enumerate the enum values, or pattern-match REVISE_STATUS in breadcrumb parsing, issue-body writing, or Step-3 stdout KV consumers. Verify whether any other scripts, docs, or test fixtures would silently treat ok-fallback as a failure or unexpected value. Check whether the plan's claim that 'the new ok-fallback value propagates into round-summary.env and into the Step-3 stdout KVs without any other consumer change' is supported by the actual call graph. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"harness-assertion-gap","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan forbids adding test cases but asserts the existing harness has no count or order assertions that would break; this claim is made without citing specific line numbers, leaving an unfounded assumption that could cause a silent test regression.","prompt_body":"The plan states 'no count or order assertion exists, only assert_line per KV' in test-revise-plan-with-waterfall.sh, but does not cite any line numbers to support that claim. Read the test script and verify whether any assertion checks the exact count of REVISE_TIER_N_STATUS keys emitted, expects REVISE_STATUS=ok (not ok-fallback) in the success-path cases, or checks that winner is empty at the end of a given test case. Also check whether test-plan-review-loop.sh stubs only REVISE_STATUS=ok and REVISE_STATUS=failed-* — if any stub would now produce ok-fallback, the plan's claim about unchanged consumer test behavior needs a correction step. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"scope-mutation-side-effects","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan mutates the script-level PATCH_FORMAT variable from unified-diff to file-replacement for tier-4 without auditing downstream uses of that variable in finalize() or KV emitters, risking misleading diagnostics that misreport the caller-requested format.","prompt_body":"The plan overwrites the script-level PATCH_FORMAT variable when entering tier-4, then calls compose_prompt and attempt_tier using the new value. Verify whether finalize(), any KV emitters, or logging functions read PATCH_FORMAT after the tier-4 block and would therefore emit PATCH_FORMAT=file-replacement when the original caller passed --patch-format unified-diff. Check whether the plan should save ORIG_PATCH_FORMAT before mutation and restore or emit it for forensics, and whether omitting this step would mislead operators reading the emitted KVs. Confirm whether compose_prompt uses PATCH_FORMAT as a branch selector or as a template variable, since the plan's description of 're-rendering with the file-replacement instruction body' implies a branch but does not cite how compose_prompt actually reads the variable. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
